1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,500
You’re sitting by the airplane window.

2
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:08,250
You look past the wing, admire the
clouds… and notice something strange.

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:10,650
There’s a tiny hole in the window.

4
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:15,480
On the ground, a hole in your window
usually means something needs fixing.

5
00:00:15,730 --> 00:00:19,700
On an airplane, it raises a
much more interesting question.

6
00:00:19,950 --> 00:00:24,414
Why would engineers deliberately put a
hole in something that helps keep cabin

7
00:00:24,454 --> 00:00:25,700
pressure inside?

8
00:00:27,750 --> 00:00:31,630
The answer starts with something you
probably can’t see from your seat.

9
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:34,120
You aren’t looking through just one pane.

10
00:00:34,370 --> 00:00:39,190
In a common passenger-window design,
there’s a protective panel nearest you,

11
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:41,730
followed by two structural panes.

12
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:47,490
The tiny hole is in the inner structural
pane—the middle layer in our illustration.

13
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:48,880
And crucially,

14
00:00:48,930 --> 00:00:52,720
it doesn’t go all the way through
the window assembly to the outside.

15
00:00:53,370 --> 00:00:55,170
That changes the mystery.

16
00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:59,780
We aren’t asking why someone made a hole
straight through the side of an airplane.

17
00:01:00,030 --> 00:01:04,540
We’re asking why one layer needs an
opening while another remains sealed.

18
00:01:05,190 --> 00:01:08,390
To understand that, we need
to talk about pressure.

19
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:12,420
As an airplane climbs, the
air outside becomes thinner.

20
00:01:12,670 --> 00:01:14,480
The cabin is pressurized,

21
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:18,000
so the pressure inside is higher
than the pressure outside.

22
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:21,680
That difference pushes outward
on the aircraft’s windows.

23
00:01:22,330 --> 00:01:25,290
Now imagine our window
layers pulled apart.

24
00:01:25,540 --> 00:01:26,890
There’s the cabin.

25
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:29,280
There’s the inner structural pane.

26
00:01:29,530 --> 00:01:31,110
Then a small space.

27
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:33,490
Then the outer structural pane.

28
00:01:33,740 --> 00:01:38,432
The tiny opening connects the cabin’s
air pressure with that space between the

29
00:01:38,472 --> 00:01:39,830
structural panes.

30
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:42,730
It’s called a breather
hole, or a bleed hole.

31
00:01:42,980 --> 00:01:47,930
As cabin pressure changes, the hole
allows pressure in that space to adjust.

32
00:01:48,180 --> 00:01:52,825
That means the inner structural pane
normally has roughly the same pressure

33
00:01:52,865 --> 00:01:54,100
on both sides.

34
00:01:54,350 --> 00:01:58,793
The outer pane carries the normal
pressure difference between the cabin and

35
00:01:58,833 --> 00:02:00,140
the outside air.

36
00:02:00,390 --> 00:02:05,160
So this little opening helps determine
which layer does the everyday work.

37
00:02:05,810 --> 00:02:07,620
Here’s a way to picture it.

38
00:02:07,870 --> 00:02:12,170
Imagine two transparent barriers
with a small room between them.

39
00:02:12,420 --> 00:02:15,460
The first barrier has an
opening into that room.

40
00:02:15,710 --> 00:02:17,880
Air can move through the opening,

41
00:02:18,050 --> 00:02:22,090
allowing the pressure in the room to
approach the pressure on your side.

42
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:25,980
The second barrier still separates
that air from the outside.

43
00:02:26,230 --> 00:02:30,710
The opening in the first barrier
hasn’t created a passage through both.

44
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:33,060
That’s the important distinction.

45
00:02:33,310 --> 00:02:37,080
The hole connects two spaces
inside the window system.

46
00:02:37,330 --> 00:02:40,540
It doesn’t give cabin air
a clear route into the sky.

47
00:02:41,190 --> 00:02:44,590
And the arrangement has
another purpose: a backup.

48
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:49,440
The inner structural pane isn’t just
there to make the window look thicker.

49
00:02:49,690 --> 00:02:54,127
Airbus explains that each of its two
structural cabin-window panes can

50
00:02:54,167 --> 00:02:58,930
independently withstand the maximum
pressure difference normally experienced

51
00:02:58,970 --> 00:03:00,210
during flight.

52
00:03:00,460 --> 00:03:02,340
If the outer pane fails,

53
00:03:02,380 --> 00:03:07,300
the inner pane is designed to take the
pressure load and maintain cabin pressure.

54
00:03:07,950 --> 00:03:11,260
That makes the window a
system with different jobs.

55
00:03:11,510 --> 00:03:14,630
One layer protects
against everyday contact.

56
00:03:14,880 --> 00:03:17,360
One normally carries the pressure load.

57
00:03:17,610 --> 00:03:19,930
Another provides structural backup.

58
00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:23,930
And a tiny opening helps manage
the pressure between them.

59
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:28,950
There is one naming detail that
can make explanations confusing.

60
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:32,850
Some descriptions call the pane
with the hole the “inner pane.”

61
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:35,130
Others call it the “middle pane.”

62
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:37,700
They may be describing the same layer.

63
00:03:37,950 --> 00:03:41,460
“Inner” distinguishes it from
the outer structural pane.

64
00:03:41,710 --> 00:03:46,790
“Middle” counts the protective panel
nearest the passenger as a third layer.

65
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:50,800
For our illustration, we’re
calling them the protective panel,

66
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:54,630
the inner structural pane,
and the outer structural pane.

67
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:58,220
Different aircraft can use
different window designs,

68
00:03:58,390 --> 00:04:03,500
but that common arrangement explains
the little hole many passengers notice.

69
00:04:04,150 --> 00:04:06,900
Next time you get the
window seat, look for it.

70
00:04:07,150 --> 00:04:12,420
Something that initially looks like a flaw
is part of a carefully arranged system.

71
00:04:12,670 --> 00:04:16,640
The surprising part isn’t simply
that the window has a hole.

72
00:04:16,890 --> 00:04:21,440
It’s that putting a hole in one layer
helps the other layers do their jobs.

73
00:04:22,090 --> 00:04:25,370
Everyday things. Surprising explanations.

74
00:04:25,620 --> 00:04:27,140
That’s Why Is That?
